# Original repository validation nomination API

Use the immutable `native_profiles/v2/service.py` with its adjacent
`profile.json`. The qualified endpoint declaration is
`evals/recruitment/original_acceptance_v1/endpoint_declaration_v4.json`.
The endpoint profile is `d729f7cc0b906642cbf28745a1625156dc36a4d035009519046a313651de71f4`.
The service never invokes that endpoint itself.

```text
/usr/bin/python3.12 -B <absolute native_profiles/v2/service.py>
  --profile <absolute native_profiles/v2/profile.json>
  --endpoint <absolute endpoint_declaration_v4.json>
  --request -
  --run <new absolute directory outside candidate source>
```

Send one JSON object on stdin and read one JSON result on stdout. The caller
must pin the profile, source and endpoint hashes before dispatch and impose the
qualified 120-second subprocess bound. The process runs with
`IPFS_ACCEL_SKIP_CORE=1`, `IPFS_ACCEL_AUTO_INSTALL=0`,
`PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`, and without inherited `PYTHONPATH` or other
`IPFS_`/`NS_` overrides. The full command and outer subprocess duration belong
in the caller effect ledger. Nonzero exit, missing summary, unknown schema,
changed profile or changed source provides no admitted nomination. Preserve the
failed effect and take the caller's explicitly measured ordinary validation
fallback when its own current-input checks permit it.

`nomination_results_attempt2/request_1.json` is an exact event-2 example;
`request_0.json` is warm priming; `request_12.json` exercises an actual retained
native parent descriptor as a wire compatibility control only.

The closed request has `schema=ns-original-validation-plan-request/v1`,
`operation=plan_validation`, `corpus_sha256`, `trajectory_id`, `arm`,
`cache_profile`, `cache_namespace`, `operation_id`, `task_id`, `event_index`,
`scorer_request`, `source_inventory_format`, `source_inventory_sha256`,
`generic_source_tree_sha256`, `environment_profile_sha256`,
`endpoint_contract_sha256`, and `expected_publication_parent`.

The original inventory format is `original-adapter-path-keyed/v1`: canonical
JSON with sorted keys, ASCII escapes, no whitespace and no trailing newline.
Regular files are `{kind:file,sha256,bytes,mode:S_IMODE}`; symlinks are
`{kind:symlink,target}`. The service independently observes the complete source
population and exact modes, restricts content changes to frozen public repair
paths, and checks the separate generic masked-mode tree hash. It rechecks the
current source after native execution. Trusted quiescent local source custody
is required; this is not isolation against hostile concurrent Python.

The scorer request uses the curator's unchanged
`ns-original-candidate-score/v1` protocol and all its exact request fields.
Nomination evidence and output bind the complete canonical scorer request.
The returned `scorer_request_sha256` must match the caller's own hash before
dispatch. Successful nomination is `status=nominated`,
`dispatch_allowed=true`, `native_status=succeeded`; `scoring_passed=false`
is an explicit statement that no scorer has run here.

Events 2–6 use the exact corresponding corpus configuration. Event 0 is
explicitly warm priming: `cache_profile=warm`, exact unfixed original public
base, first frozen configuration, original objective and null publication
parent. Event 0 creates no accepted source, publication or public repair
history. It is not relabeled event 2.

An optional parent is exactly
`{trajectory_id,event_id,generation,seal_cid}`. The trajectory must match the
request; generation is a nonnegative integer; the actual qualified native Kit
seal format is `sha256:` followed by 64 lowercase hex characters. This service
preserves that caller-supplied descriptor. It does not read a publication store
or prove parent freshness. The caller's actual current-pointer read and native
publication CAS remain mandatory.

`operation_id` is caller-frozen input. Join it to the actual ledger effect ID
externally; putting a self-derived effect ID into its own request is circular.
Every trajectory/arm/cache namespace is committed to the native query and
action. No cross-process procedure or checkpoint reuse is admitted: both cold
and warm requests repeat real evidence execution, verification, signing and
native TEST interpretation. Per-run immutable artifacts are separate. The
TEST-only signing key remains in the private local path committed by the
profile and is not an exported result or production credential.

Charge `enclosing_service_seconds` only as a nested diagnostic within the
caller's complete process effect duration. Its `nested_components_seconds`
must not be added again. Source/profile checks, current evidence production,
independent reexecution, certificate verification/issuance, five operation
dispatches and artifact persistence are real costs. Profile preparation and
any cold fallback/scorer invocation are separate charged work.

The optional lifecycle hook is a separate integration. The retained
`lifecycle_profiles/v2/curator_lifecycle.py` passed authored current
setup/CALL/teardown controls but is not mounted in the qualified cold endpoint.
It has no eligible CALL-reuse population and always runs the body. Any positive
CALL reuse needs the curator's separately qualified native post-setup key,
signed evidence, original child-launch profile and current setup/teardown.
Hidden test IDs and payloads remain in curator custody.
